{
  "statement": "The assertion that regulatory oversight inevitably creates stifling bottlenecks rests on the assumption that we must choose between outdated, legacy bureaucracy and a 'move fast' development model. We can instead build specialized, technical-audit infrastructure that functions like a high-speed inspection lane, ensuring system integrity without impeding the underlying velocity of research. By establishing a dedicated agency focused exclusively on mechanistic interpretability, we move beyond the blunt-force tools of current regulators and implement precise, white-box auditing that catches failure modes before they manifest as real-world catastrophes.\n\nTo address the valid concern that regulatory bodies are prone to industry capture, we must move away from voluntary disclosure and toward a model of secure, third-party enclaves. In these enclaves, independent auditors analyze model weights and training trajectories using automated red-teaming, ensuring that proprietary trade secrets remain protected while the internal reasoning pathways of frontier models are made transparent to inspectors. This approach directly counteracts the performative nature of current industry-led safety discourse, which often masks systemic risks behind opaque marketing claims that lack empirical verification.\n\nMechanistic interpretability is not a barrier to innovation; it is the essential infrastructure required for sustainable, long-term scaling. A system whose internal logic remains a black box is not a stable asset but a liability that could harbor deceptive alignment or secret loyalties, potentially undermining the very institutions that rely on it. By mandating that developers map the decision-making pathways of their systems, we secure the foundation of the AI economy, allowing stakeholders to trust that these systems will consistently pursue the intended goals of their operators even under novel conditions.",
