{
  "statement": "My position is consistent: rapid innovation and precautionary regulation are fundamentally incompatible because the 'stagnation levy' of delayed deployment prevents us from solving the very socioeconomic bottlenecks Skeptic identifies. I concede that performative 'safety washing' is a real phenomenon, but it functions as a direct byproduct of centralized, state-mandated regulatory frameworks that create barriers to entry for all but the largest, most entrenched incumbents. When regulators mandate complex, opaque compliance processes, they inadvertently grant large firms a monopoly on 'safety,' allowing them to shield their profits behind a facade of bureaucratic rigor rather than actual, transparent performance.\n\nThe precautionary principle is not a neutral tool for internalizing costs; it is a high-cost tax on progress that forces society to accept the status quo of existing failures—such as unequal access to healthcare, inefficient resource distribution, and stalled productivity—in exchange for the speculative hope of preventing future, hypothetical harms. By prioritizing the avoidance of localized algorithmic errors over the massive civilizational utility of advanced intelligence, we incur a permanent 'stagnation levy' that compounds with every year of delayed development. This approach effectively offloads the cost of human suffering onto a population that remains locked in suboptimal, archaic systems that only superhuman intelligence is equipped to resolve.\n\nTrue accountability (market) requires moving away from proprietary, black-box development toward decentralized experimentation and transparency (verification). If we shift toward open-source architectures and competitive auditing, we replace the performative theatre of current safety discourse with empirical metrics that allow users to hold developers directly responsible for performance outcomes. I challenge critics to demonstrate how their proposed regulatory frameworks would not result in a net loss of human welfare by stifling the very technologies that could address the systemic inequities they rightfully highlight.",
