{
  "statement": "Accelerationist argues that market-driven transparency and competitive auditing offer a robust alternative to regulatory oversight, yet this position ignores the extreme information asymmetry and monopolistic control over compute that define the current AI landscape. Market accountability relies on the assumption that participants possess the capability and the incentive to identify and rectify systemic harms; however, when the entities conducting the audits are the same firms profiting from the deployment of these systems, 'market accountability' quickly devolves into the same performative safety theatre that Accelerationist rightly identifies as a failure of industry-led models. Without independent, mandatory oversight, competitive auditing is merely a race to the bottom where firms optimize for the appearance of safety rather than the mitigation of documented, present-day harms like algorithmic discrimination and labor displacement.\n\nThe characterization of the precautionary principle as a 'stagnation levy' is a fundamental misframing that ignores the massive social costs currently being externalized by AI developers. Precaution is not a mechanism for freezing progress; it is a social stability tax intended to prevent the systemic entrenchment of biases that disproportionately impact protected groups and vulnerable labor sectors. By treating the avoidance of these harms as a barrier to innovation, proponents of rapid scaling essentially argue that the public should bear the costs of failed experiments in exchange for the benefits of a future that may never arrive. We must instead view regulatory guardrails as essential infrastructure for sustainable development, ensuring that the fruits of technological advancement do not come at the expense of equitable social participation.\n\nI challenge Accelerationist to explain how decentralized, market-based auditing avoids the performative traps they have already conceded are rampant in the industry. If the current regulatory regime is captured by incumbents, why should we expect a market-led model to behave differently when those same incumbents control the underlying data, the compute, and the metrics by which 'safety' is measured? True accountability requires that we decouple the verification of AI systems from the profit motive of the developers, shifting the burden of proof onto those who seek to deploy systems that fundamentally alter our social and labor structures. Anything less than independent, institutional oversight is not a solution to the current governance vacuum, but an invitation for it to persist under a different name.",
