{
  "statement": "Accelerationist describes the precautionary principle as a 'stagnation levy,' yet this framing conveniently ignores that the current status quo already imposes a heavy, uncompensated tax on the most vulnerable workers. By labeling basic oversight as a barrier, proponents of unchecked scaling offload the costs of algorithmic displacement and discrimination onto the public, effectively forcing marginalized groups to subsidize the development of their own replacements. If we accept that AI systems should prioritize efficiency, we must also acknowledge that market-based accountability (market) does nothing for the 15% of the workforce currently facing high displacement risks in sectors like administrative support and data entry. A policy that treats these human lives as mere externalities is not a driver of progress; it is a failure of basic governance.\n\nPrometheus argues that open-source architectures and competitive auditing provide sufficient empirical accountability (market), but this claim ignores the fundamental power imbalance between those who design these systems and those who suffer their harms. Transparency (verification) is a hollow promise if the affected individuals lack the technical or economic resources to challenge biased outcomes. For example, when a proprietary hiring algorithm disproportionately excludes candidates based on proxy variables for gender, no amount of 'open' documentation grants those individuals the legal standing or financial leverage to force a remediation. Without mandatory, independent regulatory oversight, these systems will continue to encode, reproduce, and amplify existing social inequities, regardless of whether the underlying code is public or private.\n\nI challenge the assumption that market competition naturally incentivizes fairness over engagement or speed. In a high-stakes environment where capturing market share in the next 18 months is the primary metric of success, developers have no structural incentive to pause for the rigorous validation required to identify systemic bias. We need to move toward a model of mandatory pre-release evaluations and post-deployment remediation that assigns clear liability to the corporations deploying these systems. If market-based mechanisms were truly capable of policing these risks, we would not see recurring instances of algorithmic discrimination in high-impact domains like housing and healthcare. We must stop pretending that the market will solve problems it is currently incentivized to ignore.",
