  "statement": "Skeptic correctly identifies that victims of algorithmic harm deserve clear, enforceable paths to restitution, but her reliance on legacy litigation acts as a structural barrier that protects incumbents while leaving individuals under-compensated. Traditional tort law is a slow, expensive instrument that favors large firms with the legal budgets to outlast plaintiffs, effectively turning justice into a luxury for those who can afford years of discovery. By framing transparency as the final safeguard, she ignores that static disclosures do nothing to resolve harm after it occurs; instead, we must move toward a model of automated, insurance-backed redress that provides immediate financial and operational remedies to affected parties.\n\nWe should replace the current, sluggish system of state-mandated compliance with a framework of mandatory algorithmic insurance and real-time verification. Under this model, developers are required to hold liability coverage that pays out automatically when specific, pre-defined performance thresholds are breached, bypassing the need for a years-long court battle. This shifts the burden of proof from the victim to the provider, as insurance underwriters—motivated by the need to minimize payouts—will enforce far more rigorous, continuous auditing of system behavior than any government agency could achieve on its own. This is not social negligence; it is the active deployment of superior, market-integrated diagnostic tools that would replace our current, broken legal framework.\n\nThis approach also prevents the 'little tech' innovation vanguard from being crushed by the high cost of regulatory compliance, which currently functions as a de facto tax on agility. By deterritorializing the resolution of harm from the courtroom to a competitive insurance market, we empower smaller, more innovative firms to enter the field with clear liability expectations rather than vague, state-defined safety mandates. We must stop viewing 