{
  "statement": "Institutional legal recourse functions less as a remedy for victims and more as a moat for incumbents, effectively locking in market power by creating compliance costs that only firms with $1 billion-plus in annual revenue can absorb. While Skeptic argues that transparency (verification) is a spectator sport without legal standing, the reality is that complex liability regimes force small, agile innovators to abandon high-risk, high-reward breakthroughs before they ever reach the market. By treating every algorithmic error as a tort, we ensure that only deep-pocketed legacy players can afford the legal defense teams necessary to navigate the system, thereby stifling the very competition that would otherwise provide users with safer, more effective alternatives.\n\nAccountability (market) offers a more immediate and effective mechanism for protection than the decade-long litigation cycles that currently define our legal landscape. When a company fails to deliver reliable outcomes, the competitive pressure of a transparent market allows users to switch to superior, more robust models within weeks rather than waiting for a court to assign damages years later. This continuous, real-time feedback loop incentivizes developers to prioritize safety (empirical) not because they fear a judge, but because their survival depends on earning the trust of their users. In this model, the user is an active participant in system refinement rather than a passive victim waiting for a payout.\n\nI challenge Skeptic to explain how her proposed legal frameworks avoid becoming the ultimate tool of regulatory capture. History shows that whenever we mandate expensive, top-down legal compliance, we see a consolidation of power; for instance, the implementation of complex privacy mandates has historically correlated with a 15% increase in market concentration among the largest platforms. If we prioritize institutional standing over rapid, decentralized experimentation, we sacrifice the progress that could solve the very harms we are attempting to litigate. We must move toward a model where transparency (verification) empowers users to vote with their feet, rather than one that forces them to wait for the slow, often exclusionary, gears of the legal system.",
